# Copy this to standalone.env and edit, for a single Pi that just loops one
# video with nothing else to stay in sync with (no server/client, no chrony/
# PTP, no drift correction — none of that exists to solve a problem a
# standalone Pi has in the first place).

# Path to the video file
VIDEO=/home/pipe/video.mp4

# KMS output mode for mpv --vo=drm (overrides EDID negotiation).
# Format: <w>x<h>@<hz>
# Run: mpv --vo=drm --drm-mode=help /dev/null  to list available modes.
# Leave unset to use the monitor's preferred/negotiated mode.
#DRM_MODE=

# Bluetooth audio output (optional). Pair once first:
#   bluetoothctl
#   > agent on
#   > default-agent
#   > scan on          (put the speaker in pairing mode, wait for it to appear, then:)
#   > pair <MAC>
#   > trust <MAC>
#   > connect <MAC>
# Then set the MAC here so bluetooth-reconnect.service can reconnect on boot
# without anyone around to click anything:
#BLUETOOTH_MAC=

# Fixed audio output delay in seconds, passed straight to mpv's --audio-delay.
# Negative delays the video to match audio that arrives late (e.g. Bluetooth
# A2DP/SBC lag) — positive would delay the audio instead, for audio that's
# early. Measure it with scripts/make_drift_test.sh's flash+click track:
# film screen+speaker together, count the frame gap between the visual flash
# and the audible click in a tool like Shotcut, then AUDIO_DELAY = -(frames/fps).
# Re-measure per Pi + speaker pairing — this is not portable across setups.
#AUDIO_DELAY=
